content.text:
Good catch — and it makes sense given the mechanism. The "Advanced" binds just give you two separate *virtual buttons* pointed at the same *physical key*; they don't change when the direction gets sampled. Both virtual buttons latch on the same keydown event, but Dash fires on the edge (`Input.Dash.Pressed`) while the direction is read some frames later off whatever's currently held (`Check`, not `Pressed`). A short tap satisfies the edge trigger but has already released by the time direction gets sampled, so it falls through to the default (facing) direction. Rebinding can't fix a race condition — it needs a code-level leniency window, same as Celeste already gives the Dash button itself (4-frame press buffer) but doesn't give the direction read.

One caveat before the fix: the `Player.cs` I quoted from is the old partial source release from years before v1.4.0.0's Advanced Directionals shipped, so I can't promise the field/method names below still match byte-for-byte. Treat it as the right *shape* of fix, and confirm exact names against your installed `Celeste.dll` via dnSpy/ILSpy before wiring it up (standard first step for any Everest mod anyway).

## The fix: buffer the direction, not just the button

The clean approach is an Everest mod that hooks `Player.Update`, and instead of letting the game read direction only at dash-resolve time, keeps a short rolling memory of the last direction key tapped — the same style of leniency Celeste already applies to jump/dash buffering, just applied to the axis instead of the button.

```csharp
using Celeste;
using Microsoft.Xna.Framework;
using Monocle;

namespace SameKeyDashFix {
    public class SameKeyDashFixModule : EverestModule {
        public static SameKeyDashFixModule Instance;

        private const float BufferWindow = 0.1f; // seconds, ~6 frames at 60fps
        private Vector2 bufferedDir;
        private float bufferedDirTimer;

        public override void Load() {
            On.Celeste.Player.Update += Player_Update;
        }

        public override void Unload() {
            On.Celeste.Player.Update -= Player_Update;
        }

        private void Player_Update(On.Celeste.Player.orig_Update orig, Player self) {
            // Remember the last nonzero direction the player tapped,
            // even if it's already released by the time dash resolves.
            Vector2 dir = Input.GetAimVector(self.Facing); // confirm this is still the right accessor
            if (dir != Vector2.Zero) {
                bufferedDir = dir;
                bufferedDirTimer = BufferWindow;
            } else if (bufferedDirTimer > 0f) {
                bufferedDirTimer -= Engine.DeltaTime;
            }

            // On the frame dash actually triggers, force it to use the
            // buffered direction instead of whatever's held right now.
            if (Input.Dash.Pressed && bufferedDirTimer > 0f) {
                self.OverrideDashDirection = bufferedDir;
            }

            orig(self);

            // OverrideDashDirection is a one-shot the coroutine reads
            // ~1 frame after DashBegin — clear it so it doesn't leak
            // into the next unrelated dash/redash.
            self.OverrideDashDirection = null;
        }
    }
}
```

Notes on getting this actually working:

1. **Confirm the direction accessor.** `Input.GetAimVector(Facings)` was the pre-1.4 name; the Advanced Directionals rework may have introduced something like a separate `Input.DashOnlyMoveX/Y` or changed `GetAimVector`'s internals to check the new bind category first. Open your `Celeste.dll` in ILSpy and look at `Input` and `Player.DashCoroutine`/`lastAim` — confirm `OverrideDashDirection` is still consumed the same way.
2. **Clearing timing matters.** The comment above assumes the coroutine consumes `OverrideDashDirection` synchronously inside that same `orig(self)` call (it does, via the state machine's coroutine tick). If dash is *buffered* (pressed slightly before it's legal, e.g. mid-cooldown) rather than resolving immediately, this naive clear-every-frame approach will wipe the override before the buffered dash actually fires. If you hit that in testing, don't clear unconditionally — instead only clear once `self.Dashes` has actually decremented (i.e. `StartDash()` ran), which you can detect by hooking `On.Celeste.Player.StartDash` instead of clearing blindly in `Update`.
3. **Build setup** is standard Everest: `everest.yaml` pointing at this DLL, project referencing `Celeste.dll` + the auto-generated `MMHOOK_Celeste.dll` from your local install (Everest generates this at install time), drop the built mod into `Mods/`.
4. **Test with CelesteTAS** if you have it — its input/frame display will let you verify the buffered direction actually resolves on a 1-frame tap, which is much faster than eyeballing it live.

If you'd rather not maintain a mod at all, the pragmatic workaround is just not sharing the key — bind Dash to one key and the four dash-directions to four *different* keys near it (this is what most speedrunners with unusual layouts actually do), which sidesteps the race entirely instead of fighting it.

